Z||000000||batboy||08-31-99||06:45 PM||||Since I already had a 4 cylinder Fiero and a V8 Fiero, I simply had to get a V6 Fiero too. Very logical, right? I was shopping mainly for a 1988 Formula, but was checking out any car that sounded like a bargain.<p>Well, I found a nice little one owner 1985 GT that was inexpensive and should be very easy to restore. The black paint is looking kind of faded and the clear coat has peeled off in several places, but the body is in great shape otherwise. Interior is pretty clean and supposedly the stereo system originally cost $1200 when it was installed several years ago. The engine is a professionally rebuilt 2.8 that runs very strong and sounds good with a custom exhaust system. There are low miles on the clutch. Options include four speed tranny, working a/c, sunroof, tilt, PW, PL, etc...<p>The worst thing about my new GT is the paint. Since it won't need much prep work, any ideas on how much I'll have to pay for good quality no-frills paint job? I need to start budgeting for this: $500, $1000, or more?
Z||000001||lowCG||08-31-99||07:37 PM||||So,you've decided to join us six cylinder owners,congrats.<br>Fun cars,I hope,really haven't driven mine much yet.<br>Paint can cost as much as you want it to,the best deal I've seen was by prepping most of it myself,then driving it to the Sikkens spraying bodyshop down the street for $600.
